For the UI, I'll coalesce updates on a cadence by re-rendering the active message at most every ~80ms or when the delta exceeds a threshold, plus always on events. Since the UI loop already redraws every 100ms on ticks, I'll re-render on each text event and on ticks while running. I'll add a truncation marker at the end of the live active message showing it's provisional — something like a dim "…" indicator while streaming — and the history view will show a "(partial)" label on the last entry if it's incomplete, with reasoning and long code sections collapsed by default.

For the history viewer, I'm building a `TranscriptDoc` that represents the session with metadata and message bodies (loaded lazily), rendered at a given width and theme. The view will show messages as blocks with a header line indicating the prompt and role, then the body lines, with folds replacing code and reasoning regions with summary lines like "▸ code · rust · 120 lines (collapsed)". I'll track focus state for folding and navigation, plus scroll position. For lazy loading, I'll keep a manifest-based entry list and load bodies on demand into a cache — when scrolling in the history list, I'll load bodies for visible entries plus a window around them. For the extent calculation, I'll use the byte length as a rough line count estimate when bodies haven't been loaded yet, so the scrollbar can work before full content is available.

Defining the TranscriptDoc structure with session metadata, entries containing body and status info, and the rendered rows... For the live transcript view, I'm considering a cleaner architecture: maintain the flat `Vec<ScreenLine>` for backward compatibility with existing features like the OSC52 clipboard and scroll handling, while storing the active message separately. This lets me keep all the existing test coverage intact. The active message itself will be a lightweight struct that tracks the source text, whether it's running, and which lines correspond to it — when the source changes, I just replace that range of lines by re-rendering the markdown with the current width and theme.

For the UI loop, I need to detect when the active message finishes (on Finished, PromptEnd, Failed, or AttemptStart with a later attempt number) and convert it to a completed row. This means introducing a new `MarkdownRow::Live` variant that can re-render on resize, distinct from the static `Start` variant. The resize handler already has logic to handle this kind of replacement, so I can extend it to work with the live row type.

On the streaming cadence side, I'll add a simple throttle: track the last render time and only re-render if 50ms have passed since the last update. This keeps the UI responsive without hammering the rendering pipeline on every delta. The tick loop should redraw every 100ms regardless, so the 50ms throttle ensures we're not missing responsiveness while avoiding unnecessary work.

For the history listing, I'm switching to a new `history.rs` that lists conversation directories under the log folder, reading the session manifest from JSON to extract metadata like turn counts and token usage. I'll keep the old `SessionEntry` struct with its describe method, but populate it from the new session data. Sessions without manifests or with malformed entries show up in the list as "(unavailable)" for visibility, and I'm removing the 5MiB and 4096-entry limits since the user explicitly doesn't want them—each entry just loads its small manifest file.

For the UI, I'm removing the old `transcript_lines` function and replacing `replay_lines` with a `replay_doc` that stores the conversation document. I'll add `replay_focus` and `replay_folds` state to track which message is focused and which ones are collapsed. The history overlay hint updates to show the keybindings, and when Enter is pressed, it opens the replay view with the message list.

For the replay view itself, I'm adding a "c" hotkey to continue the session. The navigation uses Up/Down to move through messages and PageUp/PageDown to scroll the viewport, with Tab/Shift+Tab to jump between foldable regions.

I'm defining the key bindings: Up/Down move focus between messages, Left/Right collapse/expand the focused message, Enter/Space toggle the fold state, and PageUp/PageDown scroll the viewport in sync with the focused message. Tab cycles through foldable regions within the document. Esc returns to the history list.

For folding, I'm implementing a FoldState struct that stores which blocks are folded at a given message index, and a render function that produces a vector of rendered rows with a FoldIndex mapping row indices back to their message and block sources.

When scrolling to keep the focused message visible, I calculate the target row range and adjust the scroll offset accordingly. After a fold or resize, I recalculate to maintain the focus visibility.

For the continuation flow, I'm adding a "Continue as a new session" menu item in the replay view that triggers a confirmation prompt — the user presses 'y' to proceed with the continuation.

The UI loop resets the current worker and stages a fresh session by sending a prompt with context. The key is using the NewSession action to clear the transcript and restart the runner, then immediately submitting the staged context prompt. This works because the prompt gets held and dispatched once the bootstrap completes.

For the context itself, I'm building a function that takes the document, formats the session metadata (including size), and constructs the prompt with an instruction to treat the old conversation as historical context and revalidate any referenced files before acting.

I need to handle truncation carefully — capping the imported prompts and answers at around 192KiB total, then marking where truncation occurs if the budget runs out. I'm also checking that only entries with final disposition (completed assistant messages) and their paired prompts get imported, skipping anything provisional or incomplete. The size calculation pulls directly from the manifest bytes field.

Now I'm working on the export feature with a CLI flag that finds the latest conversation and writes it as markdown. The exporter generates a session heading with metadata, then formats each prompt block with the user message and assistant response, using collapsible details for reasoning and adaptive fences to handle any content safely.

For reference links and footnotes, I'm keeping it simple since the terminal renderer ignores them anyway — the export just passes through the original markdown as-is to preserve fidelity. The CLI command uses `--export [PATH]` as an optional value that defaults to finding the most recent session, and I need to add a test for that flow.

I'm also checking the justfile to see what test commands are available. There's a potential name conflict since the new `session_record` module will live in `src/session_record.rs` alongside the existing `src/session.rs` and `src/session_log.rs` — the import paths are distinct enough that it should be fine.

The key constraint is that the recorder needs to be thread-safe since it's shared across turns via a Box<dyn Recorder>, and both the headless and TUI paths need to create a CompositeRecorder with SessionLog and the new ConversationRecorder. For the session ID, I'll generate it the same way as SessionLog using UTC timestamp plus PID, and the directory name will be `session-{id}` without a slug. The tricky part is that SessionLog annotates the prompt with a slug that gets reflected in the transcript file name, but the ConversationRecorder's on_prompt hook fires before the rename happens, so I need to be careful about the order of operations when wrapping both recorders in the composite.

Now I'm thinking through the manifest write mechanics: the ConversationRecorder needs to atomically write a session.json file each time an entry is published, using a temp file pattern where I write to a temporary file, sync it, rename it into place, and then sync the parent directory. Since the manifest lives at the session directory root level, I can use std::fs::rename within that directory, and for safety I'll open the directory with O_NOFOLLOW to prevent symlink attacks. I'll also clean up orphaned temp files when opening an existing session directory in case of a crash during the write.

For the entry kinds, I'm defining the manifest structure with user, assistant, reasoning, tool-call, and tool-result types, each with their own disposition values and media types.

Defining the SessionRecord struct... I'm refining the status values to include "interrupted" as a separate case alongside "completed", "cancelled", "failed", and "active". I also need to capture timing metadata like created_at and finished_at in ISO8601 format. For the tool records, I'm adding bounded preview fields with a 4KiB limit per entry, and ensuring the recorder writes to a private directory with 0700 permissions. I'll need a public function to load conversation sessions from a log directory, and I'm planning the test suite to cover the manifest roundtrip, digest handling, and the full recorder lifecycle with prompts, turns, and tool dispatches.

Now I'm mapping out the test cases: handling cancelled sessions with provisional disposition, atomic manifest writes via temp+rename to avoid partial states, orphan file cleanup, directory permission enforcement, symlink rejection, UTF-8 safety in digests, and lazy loading that doesn't read body files. I'm also adding tests for export fence lengths with long backtick runs, adaptive fence sizing, and details wrapping. For history listing, I need to verify that manifest-only reads work, malformed manifests display as unavailable, and sorting is newest first without size caps. The TUI tests cover replay keybindings like PageUp/PageDown for scrolling without j/k, Ctrl+Home/End navigation, focus movement with Enter/Space for folding, and resize clamping. I'm also checking live partial message re-rendering on text arrival, resize re-rendering of the live document, and reconciliation at completion.

For continuation, I'm staging prompts that import only final answers with size and link, rejecting provisional entries, truncating large imports, and ensuring the new session restarts. I'm also planning to update the docs by adding an implementation note to the proposal and possibly creating a new ADR documenting the JSON manifest decision and approved scope in the docs/proposals/ directory.

tool stdout:     // after a model/effort change or a new session); dispatched to the worker
    // as soon as it is installed, so a prompt never waits on the UI.
    let mut pending_submit: Option<String> = None;
    // Automatic prompt replay: when a bootstrap (model load/switch) fails and a
    // prompt is held for it, the bootstrap is retried (up to this many times) so
    // the prompt is dispatched to a fresh worker once the model becomes ready —
    // the prompt is NOT lost and needs no manual resubmit. The model selection
    // is unchanged: a load/switch is retried, not a different model chosen.
    let mut replay_attempts: u32 = 0;
    const MAX_PROMPT_REPLAYS: u32 = 3;

    // Dispatch any prefilled, auto-started prompt (for example one supplied by
    // `kvist prompt`) through the same hold/send path, and clear the input so
    // the same text cannot be submitted again.
    if let Some(text) = app.take_pending_prompt() {
        app.editor.clear();
        pending_submit = Some(text);
    }

    loop {
        terminal.draw(|frame| render::render(frame, app))?;

        // Emit any staged OSC 52 clipboard escape before drawing, so the write
        // goes through this loop's stdout handle and the buffer is flushed each
        // iteration. Copying is a terminal-side convenience, so a failure here is
        // reported but never aborts the run.
        if let Err(error) = crate::tui::app::emit_pending_osc_52(app) {
            eprintln!("copy: {error}");
        }

        // Drain loop events without blocking.
        if let Some(current) = worker.as_ref() {
            app.pump(&current.rx)?;
        }

        // Install a finished session worker (initial start, a model/effort
        // change, or a new session) and dispatch any prompt held for it. A
        // failed bootstrap is reported, not fatal: the app stays usable, and the
        // next submit or new session starts a fresh bootstrap.
        if worker.is_none()
            && let Some(receiver) = bootstrap.as_ref()
        {
            match receiver.try_recv() {
                Ok(Ok(new_worker)) => {
                    *bootstrap = None;
                    app.push_event(crate::session::Event::Note(new_worker.budget_note.clone()));
                    *worker = Some(new_worker);
                    if let Some(text) = pending_submit.take()
                        && let Some(current) = worker.as_ref()
                    {
                        let _ = current.prompt_tx.send(text);
                    }
                }
                Ok(Err(error)) => {
                    // A failed bootstrap is reported, not fatal: the app stays
                    // usable. When a prompt is held for the failed worker, the
                    // bootstrap is replayed with backoff (up to a few attempts)
                    // so the prompt is dispatched to a fresh worker once the
                    // model becomes ready — the prompt is NOT lost and does not
                    // need a manual resubmit. The model selection is unchanged
                    // (a load/switch is retried, not a different model chosen).
                    *bootstrap = None;
                    app.push_event(crate::session::Event::Failed(error.describe()));
                    if pending_submit.is_some() {
                        if let Some(selected) = app.model.clone()
                            && let Some(model) = builder.config.model(&selected)
                            && replay_attempts < MAX_PROMPT_REPLAYS
                        {
                            replay_attempts += 1;
                            app.push_event(crate::session::Event::Note(format!(
                                "the model did not become ready in time; replaying the prompt \
                                     (attempt {replay_attempts}/{MAX_PROMPT_REPLAYS}) — the \
                                     prompt is dispatched once the model is ready"
                            )));
                            if let Ok(receiver) = spawn_bootstrap(builder, model, app.effort) {
                                *bootstrap = Some(receiver);
                                app.status = "starting model…".to_owned();
                            }
                        } else {
                            app.push_event(crate::session::Event::Note(
                                "the model did not become ready in time; the prompt is kept — \
                                 press Ctrl+Enter to replay it"
                                    .to_owned(),
                            ));
                        }
                    }
                }
                Err(mpsc::TryRecvError::Empty) => {
                    app.status = "starting model…".to_owned();
                }
                Err(mpsc::TryRecvError::Disconnected) => {
                    *bootstrap = None;
                }
            }
        }

        if event::poll(Duration::from_millis(150))? {
            match event::read()? {
                CrosstermEvent::Resize(width, height) => app.resize(width, height),
                CrosstermEvent::Key(key) if key.kind == KeyEventKind::Press => {
                    match app.on_key(key) {
                        KeyAction::Submit => {
                            let text = app.take_pending_prompt();
                            if let Some(text) = text {
                                let restart = match worker.as_ref() {
                                    // A model or effort change applies to the
                                    // next prompt: the old worker is cancelled
                                    // and joined (dropped), then a fresh
                                    // session starts for the new selection.
                                    Some(current) => {
                                        current.model_id != app.model.clone().unwrap_or_default()
                                            || current.effort != app.effort
                                    }
                                    None => bootstrap.is_none(),
                                };
                                if restart {
                                    if let Some(old) = worker.take() {
                                        old.handle.cancel();
                                        drop(old.prompt_tx);
                                    }
                                    // A fresh submission gets a full replay budget.
                                    replay_attempts = 0;
                                    // An in-flight bootstrap may target a stale
                                    // selection; discard it and start fresh.
                                    *bootstrap = None;
                                    let model = builder
                                        .config
                                        .model(
                                            app.model
                                                .as_deref()
                                                .expect("submit requires a selected model"),
                                        )
                                        .ok_or_else(|| {
                                            let available = builder
                                                .config
                                                .models
                                                .iter()
                                                .map(|model| model.id.clone())
                                                .collect();
                                            Error::ModelNotFound {
                                                requested: app.model.clone().unwrap_or_default(),
                                                available,
                                            }
                                        })?;
                                    *bootstrap = Some(spawn_bootstrap(builder, model, app.effort)?);
                                    app.status = "starting model…".to_owned();
                                    pending_submit = Some(text);
                                } else if let Some(current) = worker.as_ref() {
                                    let _ = current.prompt_tx.send(text);
                                } else {
                                    // The worker is still starting; the prompt
                                    // is dispatched when it is installed.
                                    pending_submit = Some(text);
                                }
                            }
                        }
                        KeyAction::Cancel => {
                            if let Some(current) = worker.as_ref() {
                                current.handle.cancel();
                            }
                        }
                        KeyAction::NewSession => {
                            // A new session needs a selected model; deferred
                            // (no model) sessions just note that.
                            let selected = match &app.model {
                                Some(model) => model.clone(),
                                None => {
                                    app.push_event(crate::session::Event::Note(
                                        "no model selected — press Tab to choose one before \
                                         starting a new session"
                                            .to_owned(),
                                    ));
                                    continue;
                                }
                            };
                            // Replace whatever is live (a running turn is
                            // cancelled with the old worker) and start a fresh
                            // session for the selected model and effort.
                            if let Some(old) = worker.take() {
                                old.handle.cancel();
                                drop(old.prompt_tx);
                            }
                            *bootstrap = None;
                            let model = builder.config.model(&selected).ok_or_else(|| {
                                let available = builder
                                    .config
                                    .models
                                    .iter()
                                    .map(|model| model.id.clone())
                                    .collect();
                                Error::ModelNotFound {
                                    requested: selected.clone(),
                                    available,
                                }
                            })?;
                            *bootstrap = Some(spawn_bootstrap(builder, model, app.effort)?);
                            app.status = "starting model…".to_owned();
                        }
                        KeyAction::Quit => {
                            app.should_quit = true;
                        }
                        KeyAction::Idle => {}
                    }
                }
                CrosstermEvent::Key(_) => {}
                CrosstermEvent::Mouse(mouse) if mouse.kind == MouseEventKind::ScrollUp => {
                    app.scroll_up(3);
                }
                CrosstermEvent::Mouse(mouse) if mouse.kind == MouseEventKind::ScrollDown => {
                    app.scroll_down(3);
                }
                _ => {}
            }
        }

        if app.should_quit {
            // Graceful teardown before the loop exits. The worker blocks on
            // rx.recv() waiting for the next prompt, so it can only exit once
            // every prompt sender is dropped. Dropping the session handle first
            // would join() a thread that can never unblock: the only prompt
            // sender lives in the worker we are dropping. That ordering made
            // the TUI hang after it closed and forced Ctrl-C. So cancel the
            // running turn, then drop the sender to unblock recv(), and let the
            // handle (which joins) drop last. An in-flight bootstrap is simply
            // discarded: its thread finishes and drops the worker it built.
            if let Some(worker) = worker.take() {
                worker.handle.cancel();
                drop(worker.prompt_tx);
            }
            break;
        }
    }
    Ok(())
}

#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;

    #[test]
    fn startup_working_directory_rejects_missing_and_regular_paths() {
        let dir = tempfile::tempdir().expect("directory");
        let regular = dir.path().join("file");
        std::fs::write(&regular, b"not a directory").expect("file");
        for path in [dir.path().join("missing"), regular] {
            assert!(crate::config::resolve_working_directory(&path).is_err());
        }
    }

    #[test]
    fn startup_working_directory_canonicalizes_relative_and_link_paths() {
        let current = std::env::current_dir().expect("current directory");
        assert_eq!(
            crate::config::resolve_working_directory(std::path::Path::new(".")).expect("relative"),
            current.canonicalize().expect("canonical")
        );
        let dir = tempfile::tempdir().expect("directory");
        let link = dir.path().join("link");
        std::os::unix::fs::symlink(&current, &link).expect("link");
        assert_eq!(
            crate::config::resolve_working_directory(&link).expect("linked directory"),
            current.canonicalize().expect("canonical")
        );
    }

    #[test]
    fn sandboxed_work_is_multi_turn_by_default() {
        assert_eq!(
            resolve_max_turns(false, None).expect("default cap"),
            MAX_TURNS,
            "a sandboxed prompt runs multi-turn without any flag"
        );
    }

    #[test]
    fn sandboxed_rejects_any_host_turn_cap() {
        for turns in [0, 1, MAX_TURNS, u32::MAX] {
            assert!(matches!(
                resolve_max_turns(false, Some(turns)),
                Err(Error::Config { .. })
            ));
        }
    }

    #[test]
    fn host_execution_is_single_turn_by_default() {
        assert_eq!(
            resolve_max_turns(true, None).expect("default cap"),
            1,
            "host work is single-turn by default to bound privilege overuse"
        );
    }

    #[test]
    fn host_turns_lifts_the_cap_when_in_range() {
        assert_eq!(
            resolve_max_turns(true, Some(10)).expect("cap"),
            10,
            "an in-range host cap is honored"
        );
        assert_eq!(
            resolve_max_turns(true, Some(MAX_TURNS)).expect("cap"),
            MAX_TURNS,
            "the maximum in-range value is honored"
        );
    }

    #[test]
    fn host_turns_without_host_execution_is_rejected_by_resolver() {
        assert!(resolve_max_turns(false, Some(5)).is_err());
    }

    #[test]
    fn host_turns_outside_the_range_is_rejected() {
        assert!(
            resolve_max_turns(true, Some(0)).is_err(),
            "zero is below range"
        );
        assert!(
            resolve_max_turns(true, Some(MAX_TURNS + 1)).is_err(),
            "above range is rejected"
        );
    }
}

